{%- comment -%}
  ==========================================================
  ARCHIVEOS STYLE CASCADE

  Order is load-bearing. Each layer assumes the one above it:

    1. archive-tokens   values only, no selectors
    2. archive-shell    the room — canvas, type, nav, de-Shopify
    3. archive-card     artifact cards
    4. archive-vitrine  the display case
    5. archive-deck     the product page (expanded deck)
    6. archive-player   the persistent transport deck

  archive-deck.css loads after archive-vitrine.css because the
  product page reuses the vitrine's own markup and constrains the
  frame it sits in. It also carries scoped overrides of Horizon's
  commerce blocks, which must therefore come after base.css.

  archive-design.css is no longer loaded. archive-tokens.css is the
  single source of truth for design values.

  Correction (Sprint 1A audit): archive-tokens.css does NOT absorb
  all of archive-design.css. Only the 8 --archive-* colour tokens
  overlap — see the LEGACY ALIASES block at the end of
  archive-tokens.css. The 21 unprefixed tokens archive-design.css
  also defined are NOT aliased and are undefined in the live cascade:

    --space-1..7        --radius-sm/md/lg/round
    --shadow-rest/hover --ease      --speed-fast/normal/slow
    --font-display      --font-body --play-button-size --blur-md

  This is safe today: their only consumer is assets/archive-card-old.css,
  which is itself dead and unreferenced. Do NOT load archive-card-old.css
  without first restoring those 21 values — it would render unstyled.

  ruud-player.css is legacy (27KB) and styles the retired
  #ruud-global-player markup. It stays loaded for now because a
  handful of its rules still reach product/collection pages; it is
  scheduled for removal in the catalogue + player pass.
  See ARCHIVE-REDESIGN.md.
  ==========================================================
